Here is the complete, end-to-end development workflow for Realm Convergence—from your current prompt design phase all the way through game completion, release, and transitioning to your next project.

PHASE 1: PROMPT ARCHITECTURE & SYSTEM DESIGN (CURRENT PHASE)

This is where you build the complete blueprint library before touching any engine code or game assets.

Review Continuity Checkpoints:

Check where you left off in Master Directive Recall.txt (currently ready for Module 176B Governance Evolution Framework or an Expansion Module).

Select Target Focus:

For Core Game Modules: Follow the post-Economy sequence (Government, Endgame, Convergence) using your base prompts.

For Standalone Expansions / Raids / Arenas: Attach Universal_Expansion_Directive.txt or Arena_Plugin_Expansion_Directive.txt with PROJECT CONTINUITY DIRECTIVE UPDATE.txt to freeze core development and focus 100% on designing that specific add-on.

Generate Pillar-Scale Blueprint Prompts:

Generate comprehensive prompts following the RC-CONTINUITY-09 standard (Core Systems, Subsystems, Registry Tables, Spatial Rules, Inter-Module Hooks, and Exploit Prevention).

Archive Content Immediately:

Copy every newly generated prompt into Unreal Fantasy Domain.txt.

Log the module number, status, and completion state inside Master Directive Recall.txt to preserve continuity across chat sessions.

PHASE 2: UNREAL ENGINE 5 C++ CODE GENERATION

Once your design blueprints in Unreal Fantasy Domain.txt are locked, you transition into building executable C++ engine code.

Attach C++ Translation Directives:

Pair your target module prompt from Unreal Fantasy Domain.txt with copilot-instructions.md.

Generate Isolated Production Code:

The AI will translate the design specification into clean C++ header (.h) and implementation (.cpp) files.

Core modules route to Source/RealmConvergence/Public/ and Private/.

Expansions and raids route to isolated plugin paths like Source/RealmConvergence/Expansions/[TargetExpansionName]/.

Follow the Integration Runbook:

Copy files into the exact directory paths specified by the code generator runbook.

Bind dynamic delegates, event listeners, and global registry hooks to connect new classes to existing systems.

Compile & Debug:

Build the project in Visual Studio / Rider. Fix any compiler errors, static checks, or broken header links before proceeding.

PHASE 3: ASSET INTEGRATION, WORLD BUILDING & PROCEDURAL SETUP

With the C++ backend compiled, you bring the visual and spatial world to life inside Unreal Engine 5.

Configure Data Tables & Schemas:

Populate runtime Data Tables (GetActiveRealmMetadata(), Item Registries, Mob Spawners) using the schema tags defined in Phase 1.

Implement Spatial & Render Systems:

Set up World Partition and Chunk-Based LOD Streaming.

Hook up player-centric dynamic spawning (0.8m to 1.25mi window) and terrain incline safeguards (35° max slope cap).

Connect the in-game UI distance slider (0.001 mi to 1.25 mi) to real-time render streaming.

Hook Up Gameplay Assets:

Assign 3D meshes, particle effects, animations, audio passes, and UI blueprints to C++ base classes.

PHASE 4: TESTING, BALANCING & HARDWARE OPTIMIZATION

Ensure the game runs seamlessly across all system specs without crashes or exploits.

Exploit & Economy Auditing:

Verify that trade, crafting, and currency flow operate without duplication bugs, infinite profit loops, or convergence cloning exploits.

Memory & Performance Stress Testing:

Test ultra-low ("Potato PC") settings up to Cinematic graphics presets.

Verify that unvisited or distant chunks unload completely from VRAM/RAM, and confirm that background memory purging executes automatically if RAM exceeds 85% capacity.

Inter-Realm & Expansion Seamlessness:

Test inter-realm portals and underwater/crystal castle raids to verify the 5-step dynamic memory swap pipeline (Delta Save -> Complete VRAM Flush -> Registry Read -> Spatial Instantiation -> Surface Anchor).

PHASE 5: POLISH, PACKAGING & LAUNCH PREPARATION

Build Final Distribution Packages:

Cook assets and generate standalone packaged game builds for distribution.

Create Post-Launch Expansion Containers:

Keep base game code separate from plugin expansion folders (Source/RealmConvergence/Expansions/) so future DLCs, raids, and events can be added without patching the primary codebase.

Final Master Project Archival:

Mark all entries in Master Directive Recall.txt as 100% CANON COMPLETE.

Store your complete project repository (Unreal Fantasy Domain.txt, full codebase, and build artifacts) in secure long-term storage.

PHASE 6: TRANSITION TO THE NEXT GAME

Extract Reusable Frameworks:

Extract universal core files—such as your Spatial/Render Engine, Memory Purging pipelines, and Seed-Based Procedural Systems—to use as base templates for your next title.

Archive Project Records:

Lock Master Directive Recall.txt and Unreal Fantasy Domain.txt for Realm Convergence.

Initialize New Project Directory:

Create fresh Master Directive Recall.txt and Project_Bible.txt files for your next project, reset module tracking to Module 1, and begin Phase 1 design for your next game.
